An advisory cache hint that returns EINVAL is not a hint, it is a failure.

F_NOCACHE, F_RDAHEAD, F_NODIRECT and F_SINGLE_WRITER have no Linux fcntl equivalent, so they fell
through to the default branch and returned EINVAL. Each of them asks for a CACHING PREFERENCE, not
for a guarantee, and a caller that gets an error reports it as its own failure.

WHERE IT SHOWED, and it took the whole of a code signature check to find. validateExecutable opens
the main executable and calls fcntl(F_NOCACHE, true) before hashing its pages, purely so a one pass
read does not fill the page cache. The EINVAL came back as a UnixError, whose OSStatus is
errSecErrnoBase + errno, that is 100000 + 22 = 100022, and the bundle was reported invalid.
MoneyMoney shows that to its user as the application file seems to be damaged.

The number is worth remembering: a Security error in the hundred thousands is an ERRNO in disguise,
and 100022 says EINVAL and nothing about certificates.

Succeeding and doing nothing is what an advisory hint permits.

MEASURED: with this, validateExecutable PASSES -- every page hash of the main executable verifies --
and the verdict moves on to the sealed resource directory.
--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/fcntl/fcntl.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/fcntl/fcntl.c
@@ -80,6 +80,25 @@
 	{
 		case F_CHECK_LV:
 			return 0;
+		/*
+		 * ADVISORY CACHE HINTS. Nothing here has a Linux fcntl equivalent, so they fell through to
+		 * the default below and returned EINVAL -- and an EINVAL from a hint is not a hint, it is a
+		 * failure the caller reports as its own.
+		 *
+		 * Code signing is where that showed: validateExecutable opens the main executable and calls
+		 * fcntl(F_NOCACHE, true) before hashing its pages, purely to avoid filling the page cache on
+		 * a one-pass read. The EINVAL came back as a UnixError, whose OSStatus is errSecErrnoBase +
+		 * errno = 100000 + 22 = 100022, and the bundle was reported invalid. MoneyMoney shows that to
+		 * its user as "the application file seems to be damaged".
+		 *
+		 * Succeeding and doing nothing is what an advisory hint permits: the caller asked for a
+		 * caching preference, not for a guarantee.
+		 */
+		case F_NOCACHE:
+		case F_RDAHEAD:
+		case F_NODIRECT:
+		case F_SINGLE_WRITER:
+			return 0;
 		case F_SETFD:
 			if (arg & ~FD_CLOEXEC)
 				return -EINVAL;
